1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,865
There is a prison in Colorado where the window is about four inches wide.

2
00:00:04,865 --> 00:00:07,197
It is built so you can see the sky,

3
00:00:07,197 --> 00:00:08,664
but not where you are.

4
00:00:08,664 --> 00:00:13,262
Most people in the American prison system never get anywhere near it.

5
00:00:13,262 --> 00:00:13,862
Gary did.

6
00:00:13,862 --> 00:00:18,260
And Gary started out as the least dangerous man you have ever met.

7
00:00:18,260 --> 00:00:23,125
This is the story of how he fell through every single level to get there.

8
00:00:23,725 --> 00:00:24,481
Good evening.

9
00:00:24,481 --> 00:00:25,528
I'm the Archivero.

10
00:00:25,528 --> 00:00:27,506
I keep the files nobody asked for.

11
00:00:27,506 --> 00:00:29,892
Tonight's file is about a man named Gary.

12
00:00:29,892 --> 00:00:32,684
Gary isn't real, but every rule he runs into is.

13
00:00:32,684 --> 00:00:37,222
Roughly a hundred and fifty-five thousand people live somewhere on the federal

14
00:00:37,222 --> 00:00:38,618
prison ladder right now.

15
00:00:38,618 --> 00:00:42,399
Some of them walk out the front door every morning to go to work.

16
00:00:42,399 --> 00:00:45,832
Some of them have not touched another human being in years.

17
00:00:45,832 --> 00:00:48,798
The difference between those two lives is not luck.

18
00:00:48,798 --> 00:00:49,671
It is a number.

19
00:00:49,671 --> 00:00:51,300
So let's watch Gary get his.

20
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:56,800
Gary is thirty-four, an accountant, and he has just been convicted of fraud.

21
00:00:56,800 --> 00:00:58,863
He moved money that was not his.

22
00:00:58,863 --> 00:01:01,120
No weapons, no violence, no record.

23
00:01:01,120 --> 00:01:03,957
When someone is sentenced to federal prison,

24
00:01:03,957 --> 00:01:08,341
the Bureau of Prisons does not just pick a building with a free bed.

25
00:01:08,341 --> 00:01:10,082
It fills in a scoring form.

26
00:01:10,082 --> 00:01:12,468
Points for how serious the crime was.

27
00:01:12,468 --> 00:01:14,724
Points for any history of violence.

28
00:01:14,724 --> 00:01:16,465
Points for any past escape.

29
00:01:16,465 --> 00:01:19,302
Points for how long is left on the sentence,

30
00:01:19,302 --> 00:01:21,623
and for other charges still waiting.

31
00:01:21,623 --> 00:01:22,977
Gary's total is tiny.

32
00:01:22,977 --> 00:01:27,297
Staff can still adjust the number for things like gang conflicts or

33
00:01:27,297 --> 00:01:31,423
medical needs, and it keeps moving for the rest of the sentence.

34
00:01:31,423 --> 00:01:33,164
Behave, and it drifts down.

35
00:01:33,164 --> 00:01:35,034
Cause trouble, and it climbs.

36
00:01:35,034 --> 00:01:37,420
Gary's number says: minimum security.

37
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:38,516
Gary is relieved.

38
00:01:38,516 --> 00:01:40,450
Gary should enjoy this moment.

39
00:01:41,050 --> 00:01:44,558
Before we follow Gary in, look at where he could end up

40
00:01:44,558 --> 00:01:46,152
if everything goes right.

41
00:01:46,152 --> 00:01:49,404
The very bottom of the ladder is the halfway house,

42
00:01:49,404 --> 00:01:51,955
officially a Residential Reentry Center.

43
00:01:51,955 --> 00:01:53,677
Near the end of a sentence,

44
00:01:53,677 --> 00:01:56,419
people move there to rebuild a normal life.

45
00:01:56,419 --> 00:01:59,162
They go out to work, look for an apartment,

46
00:01:59,162 --> 00:02:02,605
see their families, and come back at night to sign in.

47
00:02:02,605 --> 00:02:03,179
No cells.

48
00:02:03,179 --> 00:02:03,689
No bars.

49
00:02:03,689 --> 00:02:07,707
Just a curfew, drug tests, and a staff member who knows exactly

50
00:02:07,707 --> 00:02:10,067
what time you said you would be back.

51
00:02:10,067 --> 00:02:13,192
Some people even finish the last stretch at home,

52
00:02:13,192 --> 00:02:17,146
on home confinement, with phone check-ins and surprise visits.

53
00:02:17,146 --> 00:02:18,867
That is Gary's finish line.

54
00:02:18,867 --> 00:02:22,375
All he has to do is keep his head down for a few years.

55
00:02:22,975 --> 00:02:25,434
Gary arrives at a federal prison camp.

56
00:02:25,434 --> 00:02:26,534
Minimum security.

57
00:02:26,534 --> 00:02:29,446
Dormitories instead of cells, very few staff,

58
00:02:29,446 --> 00:02:31,581
and limited or no fencing at all.

59
00:02:31,581 --> 00:02:35,981
The press likes to call these places "Club Fed." When Martha Stewart

60
00:02:35,981 --> 00:02:38,828
served her sentence at the camp in Alderson,

61
00:02:38,828 --> 00:02:44,522
West Virginia, the tabloids nicknamed it "Camp Cupcake." Gary gets a job in the kitchen.

62
00:02:44,522 --> 00:02:47,240
There are counts and rules for everything,

63
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:49,504
but the field outside is wide open.

64
00:02:49,504 --> 00:02:52,610
And one night, after a bad phone call from home,

65
00:02:52,610 --> 00:02:55,975
Gary does the one thing you must never do at a camp.

66
00:02:55,975 --> 00:02:56,881
He walks away.

67
00:02:56,881 --> 00:02:59,146
Here is the catch nobody tells you.

68
00:02:59,146 --> 00:03:01,863
Walking away from a camp is called escape.

69
00:03:01,863 --> 00:03:03,934
It is a brand new federal crime,

70
00:03:03,934 --> 00:03:05,099
and it adds years.

71
00:03:05,099 --> 00:03:07,557
Gary makes it as far as a gas station.

72
00:03:07,557 --> 00:03:10,534
By the next afternoon he is back in handcuffs,

73
00:03:10,534 --> 00:03:13,575
and his little number is not so little anymore.

74
00:03:14,175 --> 00:03:17,623
Gary's new score sends him one step up: low security.

75
00:03:17,623 --> 00:03:20,681
Here the fence appears, and it brings a friend.

76
00:03:20,681 --> 00:03:25,041
Low-security prisons have double fences around the whole perimeter.

77
00:03:25,041 --> 00:03:27,188
Gary still sleeps in a dormitory,

78
00:03:27,188 --> 00:03:30,181
in a cubicle of bunks separated by half walls,

79
00:03:30,181 --> 00:03:31,677
so privacy is a rumour.

80
00:03:31,677 --> 00:03:34,345
There are more officers than at the camp,

81
00:03:34,345 --> 00:03:36,492
and the whole day runs on counts,

82
00:03:36,492 --> 00:03:38,444
work assignments, and classes.

83
00:03:38,444 --> 00:03:42,868
Most of the people around Gary were convicted of non-violent crimes,

84
00:03:42,868 --> 00:03:44,625
just with longer sentences.

85
00:03:44,625 --> 00:03:47,097
Gary tells himself this is still fine.

86
00:03:47,097 --> 00:03:50,155
Then another man takes his seat in the TV room.

87
00:03:50,155 --> 00:03:52,953
Gary, who has never hit anyone in his life,

88
00:03:52,953 --> 00:03:55,425
decides tonight is the night to start.

89
00:03:56,025 --> 00:03:58,822
Quick question before Gary goes any deeper.

90
00:03:58,822 --> 00:04:01,879
Which level do you think holds the most people?

91
00:04:01,879 --> 00:04:04,026
Leave your guess in the comments.

92
00:04:04,026 --> 00:04:07,148
I'll give you the answer at the end of the file,

93
00:04:07,148 --> 00:04:09,750
and it is less dramatic than you'd hope.

94
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:14,001
A fight inside prison is a gift to your security score,

95
00:04:14,001 --> 00:04:15,727
in the worst possible way.

96
00:04:15,727 --> 00:04:17,918
Gary moves up to medium security.

97
00:04:17,918 --> 00:04:23,428
The perimeter gets stronger, often double fences with electronic detection systems,

98
00:04:23,428 --> 00:04:26,481
so touching the wire is not a secret for long.

99
00:04:26,481 --> 00:04:27,875
And the dorm is gone.

100
00:04:27,875 --> 00:04:30,066
Now Gary lives in a cell: a door,

101
00:04:30,066 --> 00:04:33,451
a bunk, a toilet, and a roommate he did not choose.

102
00:04:33,451 --> 00:04:36,771
Medium prisons run all kinds of jobs and programs,

103
00:04:36,771 --> 00:04:39,160
from factory work to drug treatment,

104
00:04:39,160 --> 00:04:40,953
but movement is controlled.

105
00:04:40,953 --> 00:04:42,679
You go where you are told,

106
00:04:42,679 --> 00:04:43,874
when you are told.

107
00:04:43,874 --> 00:04:47,392
For a lot of people this middle level is where a long

108
00:04:47,392 --> 00:04:50,910
sentence actually happens, year after identical year.

109
00:04:50,910 --> 00:04:53,300
Gary has just added several of them.

110
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:55,535
Here is one of Gary's days.

111
00:04:55,535 --> 00:04:57,594
Wake-up comes early, then a count,

112
00:04:57,594 --> 00:05:01,954
where every single person must be exactly where they are supposed to be.

113
00:05:01,954 --> 00:05:02,560
Breakfast.

114
00:05:02,560 --> 00:05:05,891
Work call: the kitchen, the laundry, the factory floor.

115
00:05:05,891 --> 00:05:06,739
Another count.

116
00:05:06,739 --> 00:05:07,102
Lunch.

117
00:05:07,102 --> 00:05:07,708
More work.

118
00:05:07,708 --> 00:05:09,161
The yard, if it is open.

119
00:05:09,161 --> 00:05:11,826
Mail call, which for Gary is mostly silence.

120
00:05:11,826 --> 00:05:12,249
Dinner.

121
00:05:12,249 --> 00:05:13,097
Another count.

122
00:05:13,097 --> 00:05:13,642
Lockdown.

123
00:05:13,642 --> 00:05:16,004
And then, while he sleeps, more counts,

124
00:05:16,004 --> 00:05:20,364
a flashlight beam checking that the shape in the bed is still breathing.

125
00:05:20,364 --> 00:05:23,816
Change the level and the shape of the day barely changes.

126
00:05:23,816 --> 00:05:26,057
What changes is how far you can walk,

127
00:05:26,057 --> 00:05:30,175
how many doors you pass, and how many people are watching you do it.

128
00:05:30,775 --> 00:05:34,442
Gary joins the wrong group in the yard for protection.

129
00:05:34,442 --> 00:05:36,411
Then a fight with an officer.

130
00:05:36,411 --> 00:05:40,621
Now he is sent to high security: a United States Penitentiary.

131
00:05:40,621 --> 00:05:43,812
Walls or reinforced fences, towers and patrols,

132
00:05:43,812 --> 00:05:45,713
cells for one or two people,

133
00:05:45,713 --> 00:05:49,312
and the most staff per prisoner of any regular level.

134
00:05:49,312 --> 00:05:52,232
Every movement is controlled: when you eat,

135
00:05:52,232 --> 00:05:54,337
when you walk, when you shower.

136
00:05:54,337 --> 00:05:57,460
Gary's new cellmate loves one story above all,

137
00:05:57,460 --> 00:06:00,176
the most famous penitentiary in history.

138
00:06:00,176 --> 00:06:05,337
Alcatraz, a federal prison on a rock in San Francisco Bay from 1934 to 1963.

139
00:06:05,337 --> 00:06:09,072
Thirty-six men tried to escape it in fourteen attempts.

140
00:06:09,072 --> 00:06:10,769
Officially, none made it.

141
00:06:10,769 --> 00:06:14,232
But five were never found, including three men who,

142
00:06:14,232 --> 00:06:18,646
one night in June 1962, left papier-mâché heads in their beds and

143
00:06:18,646 --> 00:06:22,041
paddled into the dark on a raft made of raincoats.

144
00:06:22,041 --> 00:06:25,300
Gary listens, and starts measuring the air vent.

145
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:27,207
He does not get far.

146
00:06:27,207 --> 00:06:31,325
Gary is caught with tools and goes to the Special Housing Unit,

147
00:06:31,325 --> 00:06:36,292
the SHU, which inmates call "the hole." Almost every federal prison has one.

148
00:06:36,292 --> 00:06:38,971
It is where people go for breaking rules,

149
00:06:38,971 --> 00:06:42,304
during investigations, or for their own protection.

150
00:06:42,304 --> 00:06:45,507
Time in the SHU means a cell for most of the day,

151
00:06:45,507 --> 00:06:48,121
meals pushed through a slot in the door,

152
00:06:48,121 --> 00:06:51,847
and recreation in something that looks a lot like a cage.

153
00:06:51,847 --> 00:06:54,003
You are not sentenced to the SHU.

154
00:06:54,003 --> 00:06:57,533
The prison can send you there any day, from any level.

155
00:06:57,533 --> 00:07:01,650
Which is why, in practice, every security level has a basement.

156
00:07:02,250 --> 00:07:07,823
In the hole, Gary loses his temper one last time and seriously injures an officer.

157
00:07:07,823 --> 00:07:11,901
And that is how a quiet accountant reaches the bottom of the

158
00:07:11,901 --> 00:07:15,571
file: ADX Florence, in Colorado, the federal supermax,

159
00:07:15,571 --> 00:07:20,328
opened in 1994 and nicknamed "the Alcatraz of the Rockies." The Bureau

160
00:07:20,328 --> 00:07:26,717
of Prisons calls this category "administrative," for prisoners considered extremely dangerous,

161
00:07:26,717 --> 00:07:32,358
violent, or escape-prone, including people who keep attacking others inside prison.

162
00:07:32,358 --> 00:07:35,348
Gary's cell is roughly seven by twelve feet.

163
00:07:35,348 --> 00:07:37,523
A concrete bed, a concrete desk,

164
00:07:37,523 --> 00:07:39,630
a concrete stool, and a shower.

165
00:07:39,630 --> 00:07:42,824
He spends about twenty-three hours a day alone.

166
00:07:42,824 --> 00:07:44,591
Food comes through a slot.

167
00:07:44,591 --> 00:07:49,688
Exercise happens alone, in a walled pen some former prisoners have compared

168
00:07:49,688 --> 00:07:51,455
to an empty swimming pool.

169
00:07:51,455 --> 00:07:54,242
And the window is about four inches wide,

170
00:07:54,242 --> 00:07:57,708
angled so he can see the sky but not the landscape.

171
00:07:57,708 --> 00:08:02,533
Over the years, the people held there have included Zacarias Moussaoui,

172
00:08:02,533 --> 00:08:05,863
Dzhokhar Tsarnaev, and Joaquín "El Chapo" Guzmán.

173
00:08:05,863 --> 00:08:08,650
There is a way back: a step-down program,

174
00:08:08,650 --> 00:08:11,708
a slow climb through several phases that can,

175
00:08:11,708 --> 00:08:16,398
after years of good behaviour, lead back to an ordinary penitentiary.

176
00:08:16,398 --> 00:08:17,825
Gary has time to try.

177
00:08:18,425 --> 00:08:20,550
So, the answer to the question.

178
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:25,965
The level holding the most people is not the supermax and not the penitentiary.

179
00:08:25,965 --> 00:08:27,267
It is low security.

180
00:08:27,267 --> 00:08:31,723
In recent Bureau of Prisons figures it holds more people than any

181
00:08:31,723 --> 00:08:34,670
other level, well over a third of everyone.

182
00:08:34,670 --> 00:08:36,932
Most people never fall like Gary.

183
00:08:36,932 --> 00:08:39,948
The ladder is mostly a long, crowded middle.

184
00:08:39,948 --> 00:08:41,319
And one last detail.

185
00:08:41,319 --> 00:08:45,363
ADX Florence is part of a federal complex that also holds a

186
00:08:45,363 --> 00:08:47,762
camp, a medium, and a penitentiary,

187
00:08:47,762 --> 00:08:50,572
side by side on the same stretch of road.

188
00:08:50,572 --> 00:08:54,274
The whole ladder, from no fence to a four-inch window,

189
00:08:54,274 --> 00:08:55,850
within sight of itself.

190
00:08:55,850 --> 00:08:58,112
Gary just cannot see it from his.

191
00:08:58,112 --> 00:09:02,773
Next time, we open the file on the strangest punishments in history.

192
00:09:02,773 --> 00:09:04,350
I'll leave the lamp on.

